# Zug0XoHVfQA · The Open World Assumption
:Source-f538e4c9-6803-54fd-9bf4-33edfc77312a a :Video ;
	:name "The Open World Assumption" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT9M22S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2024-10-21"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2024-10-21T16:39:29-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Zug0XoHVfQA"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """The Open World Assumption (OWA) (as opposed to the Closed  World Assumption (CWA)) means that we know our model is incomplete. Is this a good assumption to make? In short: it depends. In this video, we explore how to think about this in terms of your data.

OWL/RDF does the OWA by default, but we'll see in future videos different ways to enforce a CWA locally.""" ;
.

# Jx3TjC_Xz20 · Turtle Syntax
:Source-6ac61137-98d6-5d9d-96cb-20c16a927f4c a :Video ;
	:name "Turtle Syntax" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT17M4S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2024-10-24"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2024-10-24T22:39:14-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jx3TjC_Xz20"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """RDF and OWL follow in the footsteps of XML, which is a format and markup language. That syntax is not easy for humans to read, however. Fortunately, we have turtle (.ttl)! In this video, Casey walks through the structure of a .ttl file, and gives you the tools to explore writing your own.""" ;
	:cites :Source-0eaf2fbf-61f4-5b77-8980-bf9101ad996d ,	# Turtle editor and viewer (Semantechs)
		:Source-5be4e9df-c6f9-5d5c-8e78-98d7a8e0e40a ;	# RDF Turtle (W3C specification)
.

