	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RUdOTg15jDc"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """You learned about blank nodes in an earlier video. Now you get to learn one of their most important uses, OWL Restrictions. An OWL Restriction allows you to really define a class by showing what differentia distinguishes it from its superclass(es). There are many flavors and increasingly complex ways to build them, but at bottom they are pretty simple.""" ;
	:cites :Source-1e651540-a751-5b95-8e2a-49b50bfad66d ;	# Blank Nodes
.

# FT2AARaoFks · Events in OWL/RDF
:Source-1f690677-d8c8-5979-85c5-50ce2e7fc1bf a :Video ;
	:name "Events in OWL/RDF" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT22M50S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-08-06"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-08-06T06:00:28-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FT2AARaoFks"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """How do you model events and situations in OWL/RDF?
In this episode of Ontology Explained, we walk through a Davidsonian-style approach to representing actions and states using ttl (Turtle) syntax. Whether you're building a knowledge graph or designing an ontology for real-world data, this is a practical intro to event modeling in the Semantic Web stack.""" ;
.

# xKVDPrdbXDI · Categories
:Source-04639c06-9f2f-512e-bc08-59e12bb8923f a :Video ;
	:name "Categories" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT18M29S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-08-11"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-08-11T00:01:02-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xKVDPrdbXDI"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In OWL/RDF, “categories” (taking Gist's lead here) flip the usual script — instead of modeling them as classes, they’re treated as individuals. This lets you apply OWL vocabulary directly to enrich and describe them as focal entities. In this video, I explain how this approach works, why it’s used, and what it means for ontology design.""" ;
.

# devTOf4yCRw · Labelled Property Graphs (LPGs)
:Source-00e51a2d-0986-5ece-ab17-6145eb3a2b3b a :Video ;
	:name "Labelled Property Graphs (LPGs)" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT13M11S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-08-13"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-08-13T06:01:19-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=devTOf4yCRw"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In this video, I walk through the basics of Labelled Property Graphs. I use arrows.app to create an example featuring Mario to show where the L, P, and G come from.

We review the oft-cited benefits of LPGs (with the caveat that I don't necessarily agree with them!).

We also compare this with OWL/RDF ontologies, which excel at representing rich semantics, global identity, and reasoning.

Key takeaway: LPGs are great for rapid data loading and flexible querying, but OWL/RDF is more suited for formal semantics and knowledge representation.""" ;
	:cites :Source-0c17e271-a330-5bb5-ab34-3cec8d3dd23f ;	# Arrows (graph drawing app)
.

# ochIfHqtsPo · Can OWL Handle Many-to-Many?
:Source-8fb86667-0cda-53c3-b832-dc521db0da48 a :Video ;
	:name "Can OWL Handle Many-to-Many?" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT11M4S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-08-15"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-08-15T00:00:54-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ochIfHqtsPo"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In my research for the LPG video, I repeatedly ran into the claim that LPGs can handle many-to-many relationships, while OWL/RDF graphs cannot. This had me scratching my head, and so in this video I will evaluate the claim. Spoiler: it's a myth. OWL/RDF is just as good at many-to-many relationships as LPGs are; they are both graphs.""" ;
	:cites :Source-00e51a2d-0986-5ece-ab17-6145eb3a2b3b ,	# Labelled Property Graphs (LPGs)
		:Source-febc8b39-7a9a-5c4d-93f4-93fc2c063b3f ,	# RDF vs. property graphs for knowledge graphs (Neo4j blog)
		:Source-435607a0-43e1-5e24-9b54-44106e61a470 ;	# Cutting Through the Noise: An Introduction to RDF & LPG Graphs (Enterprise Knowledge)
.

# bEnOujKEae0 · Defining AI #2: Intelligent Machines
:Source-8e996d94-cdfc-58f8-a418-fd4aeba357e8 a :Video ;
	:name "Defining AI #2: Intelligent Machines" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT12M31S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-08-18"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-08-18T22:48:41-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-83871c9d-6cd9-54ad-9c0a-76864f419c1a ;	# What is Artificial Intelligence?
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bEnOujKEae0"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """After a long hiatus, this series is back on track. And for inspiration, we define AI according to the 1956 DARPA conference. This video goes through a bit of history before suggesting a combination of McCarthy and Minsky's  views: AI are machines that, were a human to do the thing they are doing, it would be counted as intelligent. I conclude that they offer great guidance for the field, but not for whether a THING counts as AI.""" ;
	:cites :Source-c13d9179-50a6-53bc-b747-1f7e244d738a ,	# Robotics and AI: An Introduction to Applied Machine Intelligence
		:Source-8ead1b9f-69e4-5a69-b402-36e6778df171 ,	# A Proposal for the Dartmouth Summer Research Project on Artificial Intelligence
		:Source-ccb04eb8-b25e-5e4a-b249-1ad64f7c9f7d ,	# Notes on McCarthy and Minsky at AI@50 (Engaging Experience, archived)
		:Source-6bde9860-3af1-5f0f-9ad9-9cec459de400 ,	# The history of the 1956 Dartmouth workshop photo (IEEE Spectrum)
		:Source-952ad1e3-76b4-5287-a4c5-279c2ea934a2 ;	# What is Artificial Intelligence? (John McCarthy)
.

