# RFBbBpDxpbs · Inference
:Source-dbb6a230-2d8a-58d8-8607-00a78f9c7db8 a :Video ;
	:name "Inference" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT17M44S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-09-05"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-09-05T00:31:45-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-f4eb1cf9-0844-5435-b06b-68625e43c943 ;	# Logic and Inference
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RFBbBpDxpbs"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """What does “inference” really mean in AI? In this video, we break down the concept step by step. We’ll define inference, contrast it with reasoning, and explore why it matters as a system-level property in artificial intelligence. Along the way, we’ll look at classic logic examples, blurred cases like diagnosis and translation, and practical tools for inference in knowledge graphs and ontologies.

What you’ll learn:
- The difference between a sentence and a proposition
- How inference works in logic and everyday AI tasks
- Degrees of inference: strength, complexity, novelty
- Why reasoning is not the same as inference
- Real-world inference tools (SPARQL, reasoners, Python scripts)

Whether you’re new to AI concepts or diving deeper into ontologies and semantic web, this video gives you the foundation to understand what inference is—and why it’s central to AI.""" ;
	:cites :Source-4d0c7f39-6bc8-5086-b434-5bbc1704a1d6 ;	# Logic Explained: The Philosophy of Arguments
.

# BdM2P4lk4Hg · LLMs Don't Hallucinate -- They Make Mistakes
:Source-4cab1a0c-e2ce-58e2-8497-3b74167bbee1 a :Video ;
	:name "LLMs Don't Hallucinate -- They Make Mistakes" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT26M23S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-09-07"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-09-07T23:07:37-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BdM2P4lk4Hg"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In this video, I argue that large language models (LLMs) don’t “hallucinate” in any literal sense. The term began in computer vision to describe useful gap-filling, but it has drifted into LLMs, where it now refers to factual errors. I trace the history of the word, give examples, and explain why the metaphor is misleading. Hallucinations are perceptual experiences without external stimuli—something only minds can have. LLMs don’t perceive the world; they generate text. Calling their mistakes “hallucinations” hides what’s really happening, feeds hype, and discourages clarity.

I consider objections, show why the analogy fails, and consider alternatives like “error,” “bug,” or “confabulation.” If we want transparency in AI, we need to stop using the wrong metaphors and call these mistakes what they are.""" ;
	:cites :Source-d64980f7-9993-5add-8442-8dfbec8b361b ,	# The history of the word "hallucination" for LLMs (joeac.net)
		:Source-b2b31cc0-9f7c-5606-b9fb-3fbb223407b9 ,	# Why AI hallucinations are worse than ever (Forbes)
		:Source-5300a764-a4ba-55d0-a7a5-d99b0e7a0d49 ,	# This Neural Net Hallucinates Sheep (Nautilus)
		:Source-83898b87-bb9b-55fb-ae89-d0af87351c11 ;	# Chris Cuomo fell for an AI video (The Present Age)
.

