# 2Q9558KrHVA · Inference in Protégé
:Source-de6caab3-4de3-516c-a0de-1322dbbf3b5c a :Video ;
	:name "Inference in Protégé" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT19M41S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-09-11"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-09-11T21:36:50-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-f4eb1cf9-0844-5435-b06b-68625e43c943 ;	# Logic and Inference
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2Q9558KrHVA"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Learn how to use Protégé with the HermiT reasoner to display inferences automatically in your ontology. In this tutorial, we walk through a simple example with Hammers and Tools, showing how class restrictions and property values can lead to inferred knowledge.

You’ll see how:
- A hammer, defined as a subclass of tools, is inferred to be a tool.
- An equivalence axiom with an owl:Restriction allows Protégé to infer new property values (e.g., a hammer’s primary function is hammering nails).
- Protégé displays both asserted facts and inferred facts clearly once the reasoner is enabled.

This is a great starting point if you’re learning description logics, OWL reasoning, or just want to make the most of Protégé’s built-in reasoning capabilities.""" ;
	:cites :Source-dbb6a230-2d8a-58d8-8607-00a78f9c7db8 ,	# Inference
		:Source-a24bffc6-2317-5d01-869d-c5ca5c3ce255 ;	# protege_inference_demonstration.ttl
.

# ouQdH9m7qVU · The Spectrum of Artificial Intelligence: Def'n #4
:Source-07c8e02a-438e-53bc-b7df-20c3fdc33e66 a :Video ;
	:name "The Spectrum of Artificial Intelligence: Def'n #4" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT16M47S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-09-15"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-09-15T05:01:36-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-83871c9d-6cd9-54ad-9c0a-76864f419c1a ;	# What is Artificial Intelligence?
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ouQdH9m7qVU"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In this video, we break down the idea of spectrum definitions — from weak vs. strong AI, to narrow vs. general, to AGI and superintelligence. Spectrum definitions sound inclusive and thought-provoking, but do they actually tell us what AI is?

I argue that spectrum definitions often collapse into circular, ad hoc labels. Instead of providing a consensus definition, they function more like marketing tools than solid conceptual frameworks.

If you’ve ever wondered why defining AI is so slippery, or why every debate seems to stretch along yet another spectrum, this video is for you.""" ;
.

# 6pAyt2Xx7-g · Fallacies Explained: Why Bad Arguments Fail
:Source-b71dbc98-205b-576c-a49f-4f9279dc82f6 a :Video ;
	:name "Fallacies Explained: Why Bad Arguments Fail" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT12M22S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-09-17"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-09-17T05:00:23-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2893b7ee-72eb-5af3-818f-2005735c8dff, :Source-f4eb1cf9-0844-5435-b06b-68625e43c943 ;	# Fallacies; Logic and Inference
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6pAyt2Xx7-g"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In this video, I define fallacies as argumentation anti-patterns — the bad structures or styles of reasoning that break the rules of good argumentation. A fallacy makes an argument flawed, just like bad form makes a basketball shot less likely to go in.

We’ll look at how fallacies work, why they matter, and why it’s best to be charitable when talking about them — sometimes the flaw is the fallacy, sometimes the whole argument gets called the fallacy.

The takeaway:
- As rational people, we want to reliably draw true conclusions from our existing beliefs.
- Logicians have studied what makes arguments strong, and also what makes them weak.
- Fallacies are the anti-patterns of argumentation — mistakes that lead us astray.

This video sets the stage for future episodes, where we’ll break down individual fallacies such as affirming the consequent and ad hominem.""" ;
.

# wISFJAj-2yc · SPARQL Inference Made Easy
:Source-62905ea1-4756-5532-b4ec-26fb7ada6269 a :Video ;
	:name "SPARQL Inference Made Easy" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT17M38S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-09-19"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-09-19T04:01:51-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-f4eb1cf9-0844-5435-b06b-68625e43c943 ;	# Logic and Inference
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wISFJAj-2yc"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In this video, I demonstrate how to use SPARQL CONSTRUCT queries as a simple rule-based inference engine. Each CONSTRUCT query works like an IF–THEN rule, where the WHERE clause defines the condition (IF) and the CONSTRUCT clause defines the conclusion (THEN).

Using a few toy examples, we explore how to:
- Infer that male siblings are brothers
- Infer that owned animals are pets
- Deconstruct an IRI to unpack semantic information

This hands-on approach shows how you can extend your ontology with custom inference rules directly in SPARQL. Perfect for anyone working with RDF, knowledge graphs, or semantic web applications.""" ;
.

# cSDA1uhNu7g · Let's Build a Chess Ontology (Part 3)
