.

# DFXU7lViBeU · The Secret to Type-Level Claims: Avoiding Logic Errors in OWL
:Source-f06e072f-29c7-512c-bcb4-310ca533ded2 a :Video ;
	:name "The Secret to Type-Level Claims: Avoiding Logic Errors in OWL" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT21M9S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-10-10"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-10-10T04:00:32-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DFXU7lViBeU"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """How to Make Type-Level Claims (e.g., Casey likes Nachos)

It seems simple enough: you want to state a relationship between an individual (like your friend Casey) and a whole class of things (like the concept of Nachos). But in ontology, connecting an individual to a class using a standard object property is a fast track to logical contradictions. This is the Type-Level Claim Problem.

In this video, we break down why statements like \":Casey :likes :Nachos\" break your formal system, and, more importantly, we give you three reliable ways to solve it.

We explore three architectural solutions for making type-level claims:

1.  Punning: Using the same name to refer to two different things (a class and a temporary individual). It's simple but can confuse your users.
2.  Annotation Properties (Recommended): Using a non-logical property to make the connection. This is our preferred method for its clarity and preservation of logical consistency.
3.  Paradigm Instances: Creating a stand-in individual that represents the class itself. It's logically sound, but adds clutter to your knowledge graph.

By the end of this video, you'll not only understand the problem but be equipped to make the choice that best suits the structure and goals of your own ontology.

For Practitioners & Students: We discuss the technical trade-offs of each method regarding logical consistency, reasoner performance, and data clarity.""" ;
	:cites :Source-6d7661b9-cd1d-5363-8db8-f66ab826b305 ;	# type_level_foods.ttl
.

# TbWCfLqYIzM · So,  you want to be an ontologist? Here's what you do.
:Source-3487057d-9b4a-53d8-a4c0-738407ec5b42 a :Video ;
	:name "So,  you want to be an ontologist? Here's what you do." ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT18M43S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-10-14"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-10-14T23:48:32-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-13344f10-72a7-5576-b24a-275dd10386cb ;	# Becoming an Ontologist and Leaving Academia
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TbWCfLqYIzM"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Welcome back to the career series! In this second video, we're diving deep into the practical steps you can take to become an ontologist. I break down the three primary paths to starting a career as an ontologist—from traditional schooling to job training and the challenging, but rewarding, self-taught route.

What you'll learn in this video:

The Three Paths: Detailed pros and cons of job training, university programs, and self-study.

Essential Skills: Which academic backgrounds (CS, Library Science, Philosophy, etc.) give you a head start.

Actionable Steps: The best books to read, the software you need to download, and the first project you should tackle to start building your portfolio.

The Three Paths to Becoming an Ontologist:

Path 1: Job Training & Pivoting (Consultancies, large tech companies)

Path 2: Academic Schooling (Mentioning key programs like UW, SUNY Buffalo)

Path 3: The Self-Taught Road (Driven by enthusiasm and self-pacing)""" ;
	:cites :Source-f4a8b982-7c63-5f68-85a5-06423f61fcbf ,	# Demystifying OWL for the Enterprise
		:Source-dcbeaf8b-7175-55e6-837f-3e6f865da981 ,	# Building Ontologies with Basic Formal Ontology
