		:Source-0dc0bbd3-8f00-5420-90b1-58d246e0095d ;	# How I Became An Ontologist
.

# n14qsp2C9Tw · Don't Be a Sheep: The Ad Populum Fallacy Explained
:Source-f86f5cdd-a9ef-5a0d-bf3e-ac58088d1d51 a :Video ;
	:name "Don't Be a Sheep: The Ad Populum Fallacy Explained" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT17M2S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-10-15"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-10-15T23:33:12-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2893b7ee-72eb-5af3-818f-2005735c8dff ;	# Fallacies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n14qsp2C9Tw"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Welcome to the next installment in our Logic and Fallacies series!

In this video, we break down the Ad Populum Fallacy, also known as the Appeal to Popularity or Appeal to the People. This is one of the most common mistakes in reasoning, where the popularity of a belief is mistakenly taken as evidence that the belief is true.

From popular political opinions to social conventions and even historical scientific beliefs (like the pre-Copernican view of the universe), we show you why the crowd isn't always right, and how to spot this flawed logic.

What you'll learn in this video:
- The standard form of the Ad Populum argument: All/Most people think X, therefore X is true.
- The difference between popularity and truth.
- Key Exceptions where popularity does matter (e.g., word definitions, elections, and social conventions).
- How the fallacy is used to justify immoral or outdated social structures.

Don't let the allure of being like everyone else keep you from forming your beliefs for the right reasons.""" ;
	:cites :Source-b71dbc98-205b-576c-a49f-4f9279dc82f6 ,	# Fallacies Explained: Why Bad Arguments Fail
		:Source-3a9683c6-4e59-5ea9-9a98-59af1197de89 ;	# The Principle of Charity: The Cure for the Straw Man Fallacy
.

# A-uCszXIijI · OWL Punning: Make Sure Your Ontology Isn't a Joke
:Source-705cb265-1e5f-559b-b7bd-42d30eefcc5f a :Video ;
	:name "OWL Punning: Make Sure Your Ontology Isn't a Joke" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT18M50S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-10-16"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-10-16T23:25:08-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A-uCszXIijI"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In the world of the Semantic Web, a single identifier (IRI) is usually meant to denote only one thing. But what happens when you use the same IRI for two completely different kinds of entities—like an owl:Class and an owl:Individual? That's an OWL pun, and it's where the logical world of OWL DL (Description Logics) clashes with the flexible world of OWL Full.

We'll define the linguistic pun (starting with a terrible post office joke), then tackle the technical pun. We'll explore exactly why these meta-level conflicts break OWL DL reasoners like HermiT and Elk, and what the true cost is—you're forfeiting the guarantees of decidability.

But don't stress! Punning isn't a victimless crime, but it's not a modeling catastrophe either. We'll wrap up with practical advice on when you should be concerned, how to keep your models consistent, and when you can afford to \"pun-t\" (sorry!).""" ;
.

# xeJNF5lQIjs · Beyond True/False: A Practical Guide to Probabilistic Modeling in OWL and RDF
:Source-412e3a7c-dbbd-5330-a07c-2eaa82a34d92 a :Video ;
	:name "Beyond True/False: A Practical Guide to Probabilistic Modeling in OWL and RDF" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT20M39S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-10-20"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-10-20T23:01:06-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xeJNF5lQIjs"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Probability seems fundamental to intellectual rigor, yet modeling it in deterministic systems like the Web Ontology Language (OWL) presents a significant challenge. OWL is designed for propositions that are true or false—full stop—which clashes with real-world uncertainty (like a \"30% chance of rain\" or \"98% data accuracy\").

This video explores practical solutions for integrating confidence, credence, and probabilistic scores into your knowledge graph. We use the following techniques:

1. Datatype Properties to connect events to decimals.
2. Named graphs to classify reliability of data sources.
3. RDF*/RDF1.2 for qualifying the probabilities on specific triples.
4. Lists to add a fourth relata, which lets us sneak in a decimal on another triple.

We'll also touch on the deep philosophical interpretations of probability, the connection to Causal Diagrams and Bayesian Networks, and look at alternative approaches like PR-OWL and BayesOWL. Learn how to bridge the gap between deterministic logic and the messy, probabilistic reality of your data.""" ;
.

# G9EG2PN7PDk · Ontology Stitching: How to Align/Merge Enterprise Knowledge Graphs (A Practical Guide)
:Source-b40d178e-9bcc-500b-b8b7-9db9678cac0c a :Video ;
	:name "Ontology Stitching: How to Align/Merge Enterprise Knowledge Graphs (A Practical Guide)" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT26M10S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-10-23"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-10-23T16:04:32-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G9EG2PN7PDk"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In complex organizations, the same word can mean two completely different things to two different teams—this is Semantic Ambiguity, and it will break your knowledge graph. This video explores the concept of \"Ontology Stitching,\" which is the critical process of taking two disparate or conflicting ontologies (like a Sales CRM's definition of \"Client\" vs. an Engineering system's definition of \"Client\") and carefully merging them into a unified, non-contradictory whole.

You will learn:
- Why terms like \"Client,\" \"Asset,\" or \"Engagement\" create homonymy problems in enterprise data.
- The difference between Ontology Mapping (finding the links) and Ontology Alignment (formalizing the relationship).
- How to use vocabularies like SKOS (Simple Knowledge Organization System) and owl:sameAs to create durable \"stitches\" that clearly define relationships like equivalence, broader, and narrower terms, preventing costly data integration errors and logical contradictions.

This is essential viewing for anyone working in Data Governance, Enterprise Architecture, or Semantic Web modeling.""" ;
.

