In the next video, we'll show how to take a chess opening structured data dataset and do some ETL to put it in the ontology/graph. Should be fun!""" ;
.

# QXCAGStCOMA · I'm Sick of the ASI Fear-Mongering (Hank Green's Video Made Me Rage, Featuring Nate Soares)
:Source-bbb894c1-cee0-5be5-8656-6af1a15854b4 a :Video ;
	:name "I'm Sick of the ASI Fear-Mongering (Hank Green's Video Made Me Rage, Featuring Nate Soares)" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT1H10M56S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-11-03"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-11-03T05:01:01-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QXCAGStCOMA"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """I watched a video with Hank Green and Nate Soares discussing the existential threat of Artificial Super Intelligence (ASI), and honestly, I was infuriated. Soares, co-author of If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies, argues that if an ASI is not perfectly \"aligned\" with human values, it will surely lead to our doom. But as I break down their major points—from their vague, fear-inducing definition of ASI to their outlandish claims about current AI capabilities—it seems like the real problem isn't a malicious super-intelligence, but rather the unnecessary mystery and power concentration surrounding the technology.

This video is my detailed objection to the \"stop all AI\" argument. I challenge the notion that LLMs are plotting, self-aware, or \"caring\" in a way that poses an existential threat. The metaphors they use, like comparing AI development to alchemy, only serve to obscure the technology and increase the power of the builders (the \"alchemists\"). Ultimately, the true \"alignment problem\" isn't with an uncaring algorithm, but with the huge corporations and influential figures who are shaping this powerful technology. I propose four foundational principles for thinking about AI that prioritize clarity, human flourishing, and a healthy distrust of the powerful.""" ;
	:cites :Source-e5582d53-237f-501c-aebd-3faeff8cb2cb ,	# ChatGPT isn't Smart. It's something Much Weirder
		:Source-0c2237de-7a3e-504b-9048-71b5de8bc6f4 ;	# Claude Sonnet 4.5 System Card
.

# R6gYZ5B4lqs · Why I Use Emacs for Ontologies (and How to Memorize a HUGE Knowledge Graph) [AMA #1]
:Source-30a5547e-4d0a-53b8-b39b-a938f3219816 a :Video ;
	:name "Why I Use Emacs for Ontologies (and How to Memorize a HUGE Knowledge Graph) [AMA #1]" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT19M30S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-11-04"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-11-04T05:01:02-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R6gYZ5B4lqs"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Why would an Ontology Engineer skip tools like Protege and TopBraid Composer and stick with a 'plain' text editor like Emacs? And how do you manage a complex knowledge graph with thousands of classes and properties without relying on memory alone? Those were two great questions from FredBlok4308, and I'm answering them in this video.

I dive into my personal workflow, explaining the history and practical reasons for choosing a text-based approach (hint: it's a historical happy accident that was supported by it being fun, fully customizable, and better than the other options I tried). I also reveal the secret to taming graph complexity, which involves good modular design, the \"use it or lose it\" principle, and leveraging SPARQL queries to serve as your method for learning the vocabula. Whether you're a Semantic Web veteran or just starting with knowledge graphs, this covers the critical strategies for scaling your data modeling efforts.""" ;
.

# 2XM8Vnoayt0 · The Philosophers Fallacy!? (Let's call it 'Blueprint Bias')
:Source-8655b9ee-8717-5fa1-bc04-3676a81a4f96 a :Video ;
	:name "The Philosophers Fallacy!? (Let's call it 'Blueprint Bias')" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT14M49S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-11-05"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-11-05T05:00:05-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2893b7ee-72eb-5af3-818f-2005735c8dff ;	# Fallacies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2XM8Vnoayt0"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """I stumbled across a video where the talked about the \"Philosopher's Fallacy\". I had never heard about it before. And being a philosopher and someone who is passionate about (avoiding!) fallacies, I had to check it out. This video unpacks what the fallacy is supposed to be. I think discuss what the process should be in creating a new, previously-unnamed, fallacy. And I dig into what is really going on with the philosopher's fallacy.

In the end, I conclude that there are some other more specific named fallacies we could use to identify the errors, so it's probably not worth adding this arrow to our quiver. But if we did, I'd call it the Blueprint Bias rather than a philosopher's fallacy.""" ;
	:cites :Source-b5ffbf17-2339-5ecb-bd27-956dc5f4a259 ,	# The Case Against Superintelligence | Cal Newport
		:Source-bbb894c1-cee0-5be5-8656-6af1a15854b4 ,	# I'm Sick of the ASI Fear-Mongering (Hank Green's Video Made Me Rage, Featuring Nate Soares)
		:Source-b71dbc98-205b-576c-a49f-4f9279dc82f6 ;	# Fallacies Explained: Why Bad Arguments Fail
.

# dh0aAYRdi0U · How to Pivot Into Ontology: Jobs, Skills, and the Future of the Field
:Source-7a38531b-50f7-5990-8cea-083338bd14cc a :Video ;
	:name "How to Pivot Into Ontology: Jobs, Skills, and the Future of the Field" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT16M48S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-11-06"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-11-06T05:01:30-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-13344f10-72a7-5576-b24a-275dd10386cb ;	# Becoming an Ontologist and Leaving Academia
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dh0aAYRdi0U"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In this video, I respond to a viewer asking whether a mid-career pivot into ontology is realistic without a strong background in logic, data science, or quantitative methods. I walk through what skills actually matter, why qualitative thinkers — especially philosophers and religious studies scholars — are often better prepared than they realize, and what the retraining curve looks like in practice. Age isn’t a blocker, and a lack of formal logic training isn’t disqualifying; the real question is how willing you are to learn some technical basics while leveraging the strengths you already have.

I also cover the job landscape: where ontology and taxonomy roles actually hide, what search terms to use, what entry-level positions look like, and how to find “learn-on-the-job” opportunities. Then I zoom out to whether the field has a long-term future (short answer: yes), how AI affects these roles, and what adjacent fields — from Graph RAG to information architecture — make good alternatives or stepping stones. If you're trying to figure out whether ontology is a viable next career chapter, this video maps out the terrain.""" ;
.

# GX31wVDz_Eg · Newcomb’s Problem Explained: One Box Or Two?
:Source-7397576e-2ebd-5804-91d8-e85789d8cf30 a :Video ;
	:name "Newcomb’s Problem Explained: One Box Or Two?" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT30M4S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-11-07"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-11-07T05:00:35-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GX31wVDz_Eg"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Newcomb’s Problem is one of the strangest and most influential puzzles in philosophy, decision theory, and AI. Should you take one box or two? Really, this is a deviant case where it pays to be irrational.

In this video, I walk through the classic setup from Robert Nozick’s 1969 paper Newcomb’s Problem and Two Principles of Choice. We cover the arguments for one-boxing and two-boxing, explain why two-boxing is ultimately the right answer, and explore why Newcomb’s Problem is really a test for decision theories—not your intuition.

What’s inside:

- The case for one-boxing
- The case for two-boxing
- Why Newcomb’s Problem pays you to be irrational
- How decision theory works (utilities, dominance, evidential vs. causal decision theory)
- Why philosophers split almost evenly on this puzzle
- Why decision theorists overwhelmingly favor two-boxing
- How Newcomb’s Problem functions like the Trolley Problem—great for clarity, bad for obsessing over

I'll also offer explanations for why this seemingly cut-and-dried problem still makes waves and controversy.

Bottom line:
Newcomb’s Problem is cool, culturally sticky, and great for thinking—but the choice itself is easy. Take both boxes (but hope/act in ways that will get the predictor to make a mistake).""" ;
	:cites :Source-cbedfcd7-dd46-5e43-a2da-a780e093108e ,	# Decision Theory (Boris Kment)
		:Source-8af92397-ccaf-5a4d-a3c7-a9d01a573dcc ,	# Causal Decision Theory (PDF on andrewmbailey.com)
		:Source-ef15c554-687a-5b52-a2b5-0e611b65998f ,	# Some Counterexamples to Causal Decision Theory (Andy Egan)
		:Source-ab0b51a8-38c7-556d-a6bd-df18a24246de ,	# Newcomb's Problem and Two Principles of Choice
		:Source-26b0cb0b-07b1-59a5-a6a5-776c623c32af ,	# Newcomb's problem (Wikipedia)
		:Source-608bea38-7f80-5821-8ea2-ec504c1d4931 ;	# Causal Decision Theory (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)
.

