# J70hmeMhNXQ · How Much Does Your Ontology Really Know?
:Source-5f93c8a1-d71c-541f-93b9-dd9ac2a00950 a :Video ;
	:name "How Much Does Your Ontology Really Know?" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT15M25S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-11-17"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-11-17T13:14:23-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J70hmeMhNXQ"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """“How complete is your ontology?”
“What does your ontology know about oil and gas?”
“Can you quantify it?”

These are some of the first questions clients, managers, and stakeholders ask me — and they usually expect a nice clean number like “83%.” But ontology completeness is way more complicated than that. In today’s video, I break down how to properly approach this question, using a metaphor everyone understands: How do you judge what a fifth grader knows?

In the video I walk through:
-  Why “counting facts” (nodes, edges, depth, connectedness, instance data) only gets you so far
-  Why time spent (dev time + application run time) matters — but is also misleading
-  Why the real answer is in your tests, applications, and use-case work products
-  How competency questions and regression tests map directly to ontology evaluation
-  How to pick a strong, narrow, connected use case and build your story around it
-  Why you should be suspicious of anyone who claims ontology completeness is simple

By the end, you’ll see why evaluating an ontology is a lot like evaluating whether a kid is ready for the next grade: metrics help, but they can be gamed. What really matters is whether the ontology performs across a representative set of tasks.""" ;
.

# lWDABXVMzHA · The Red Herring Fallacy: Don’t Get Distracted!
:Source-633fb0ad-a7b1-5a0f-821f-5ef8d0f2a93a a :Video ;
	:name "The Red Herring Fallacy: Don’t Get Distracted!" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT19M16S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-11-19"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-11-19T14:48:02-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2893b7ee-72eb-5af3-818f-2005735c8dff ;	# Fallacies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lWDABXVMzHA"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In this video, we break down the red herring fallacy — one of the most common (and most misused) fallacies in everyday arguments. We’ll look at what a red herring actually is, where the term comes from, how it shows up in modern debates, and why identifying it can get tricky in real-world conversations.

What we cover:

- The definition of a red herring
- The 1807 William Cobbett origin story
- Classic examples (including ad hominem & appeal to tradition)
- Political examples using the Epstein files discussion
- Why irrelevant details aren’t always fallacious
- The tension between exegesis and steel-manning
- How red herrings differ from straw men
- Takeaway: A red herring fallacy occurs when irrelevant information is used to distract from — or covertly support — a conclusion.""" ;
	:cites :Source-1a2bf767-717c-5f29-9962-18c707a91b8c ,	# Red herring (World Wide Words)
		:Source-c0875d14-001e-5a6c-a1ce-e0c195991bbf ,	# WATCH: Leavitt says Boebert meeting over Epstein files shows Trump administration ‘transparency'
		:Source-68e1d34d-84ba-516f-b42a-3aae04bd5dc3 ;	# Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt's full comments on Jeffrey Epstein email release
.

# ny363vb1ByE · Goals: Are they for LLMs, Cats, and Thermostats?
:Source-66f7a011-c905-5466-9bd5-54fe30a8f631 a :Video ;
	:name "Goals: Are they for LLMs, Cats, and Thermostats?" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT19M26S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-11-24"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-11-24T05:01:41-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ny363vb1ByE"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """We often hear that the biggest risk from super-intelligent AI is that their \"goals\" won't align with human goals. But what if we're asking the wrong question?

This video takes philosophical look into the concept of goals itself. We move beyond motivational speeches to explore the fundamental question: When we attribute a goal to a thermostat, a chess engine, or an LLM, are we talking about the same thing as a human goal?

We analyze prominent definitions of intelligence—like John McCarthy's view that intelligence is goal-directed computation—and use classic philosophical tools to dissect the problem, including:

- Propositional Attitudes
- Direction of Fit (Mind-to-World vs. World-to-Mind)
- Value Theory (Intrinsic vs. Extrinsic Goals, from Aristotle to Mill)

By examining goals in everything from hunger and cats to hammers and mushrooms, we develop a better framework for understanding the true nature of AI intelligence.

Do LLMs, Reinforcement Learning Agents, and Chess Engines truly have goals? Or do they have something else entirely?

Find out why the answer to this philosophical question is essential for tackling the biggest risk in modern AI: the Alignment Problem.""" ;
.

# nTcsWL_-jj0 · The Alignment Problem Explained
:Source-63c6374f-ab39-58f2-92bc-1ce0fc9e91d8 a :Video ;
	:name "The Alignment Problem Explained" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT21M48S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-11-26"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-11-26T19:28:13-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nTcsWL_-jj0"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In this video, I break down what “alignment” really means. Instead of treating AI alignment as a vague, binary idea, I argue that we need a richer vocabulary for how goals relate: complementary, compatible, in tension, and incompatible. Using examples from sports, everyday life, and human–AI interactions, I show how alignment problems arise, why they matter, and what this tells us about the Alignment Problem in AI.
We’ll look at definitions from Christian, Gabriel, Russell, and the Asilomar Principles, then refine them into a clearer framework for thinking about goals, conflict, cooperation, and the real challenges ahead in building aligned AI systems.""" ;
	:cites :Source-a2b1de5c-ccb8-536d-9e50-bf4959046533 ,	# The Alignment Problem (Brian Christian): introduction and chapter 1
		:Source-47dcb470-b73f-5966-a9bf-f23a5f039f68 ,	# Artificial Intelligence, Values and Alignment
		:Source-15e470b0-a5a7-5a7c-82db-64d82a50975c ,	# Human Compatible
		:Source-92aaa65a-d777-5bf3-9cf4-98d99d3b4711 ;	# Asilomar AI Principles
.

# 1veF-1TNFsM · The T-Box/A-Box Myth — And What Quine Can Teach Ontologists
:Source-b8597e92-cdcc-598e-8327-d71f2c72530c a :Video ;
	:name "The T-Box/A-Box Myth — And What Quine Can Teach Ontologists" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT20M1S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-12-03"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-12-03T05:00:05-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1veF-1TNFsM"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Ontologists love distinctions… maybe a little too much. One of our most sacred ones — the T-Box vs. A-Box — might actually be an illusion. In this video, I argue that there is no such thing as the T-Box, at least not as a deep metaphysical or architectural category.

We review the standard definitions (Gruber 1993; KL-One; Giacomo & Lenzerini 1996), look at examples like “BasketballPlayers play basketball” vs. “LeBron is a BasketballPlayer,” and compare this to W.V.O. Quine’s famous attack on the analytic/synthetic distinction in Two Dogmas of Empiricism (1951).

Quine’s “web of belief” shows why sharp lines between meaning-statements and world-statements don’t hold up. Ontologies work the same way: A-Box facts can force revisions in T-Box structure, classes and individuals are conventional, and everything sits on a spectrum of centrality rather than in separate boxes.

The T-Box/A-Box split is still a useful heuristic, but not a deep distinction. Think of your ontology as a web, not a two-box architecture.""" ;
	:cites :Source-dd90e597-083d-5b43-919f-b1db91e54f83 ,	# Two Dogmas of Empiricism
		:Source-41a55685-3884-5185-ac57-8aa6dd84075d ,	# A Translation Approach to Portable Ontology Specifications
		:Source-ba87681f-9816-5146-8998-d9ba4dc87bce ,	# TBox and ABox Reasoning in Expressive Description Logics
		:Source-f25b012e-b7ac-5236-97dc-6d38d160392f ;	# What Are the TBox, ABox, and CBox in a Knowledge Graph?
.

# Jzbe9J3x-6c · AI Explained: A Discipline, Minds, and the Messy Third Definition
:Source-d994ada1-b0d8-5b34-94c4-310d92455694 a :Video ;
	:name "AI Explained: A Discipline, Minds, and the Messy Third Definition" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT20M9S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-12-05"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-12-05T16:35:50-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-83871c9d-6cd9-54ad-9c0a-76864f419c1a ;	# What is Artificial Intelligence?
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jzbe9J3x-6c"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In this video, I break down the three definitions of Artificial Intelligence:
AI1 — the field dedicated to creating non-biological minds.
AI2 — the non-biological minds themselves.
AI3 — the catch-all idea of “cool tech that seems intelligent.”
The first two give us clarity. The third explains everyday usage—but it’s vague, easily exploited, and dangerous to use in policy or law. Let’s make our language about AI precise.""" ;
.

# gSSHj9-e6Mc · Ontology FAQs: Five Questions I Always Get Asked
:Source-d58d6665-fab3-5269-aaf0-22ce319d8fc1 a :Video ;
	:name "Ontology FAQs: Five Questions I Always Get Asked" ;
