# -tLlqtoqMDI · Pascal’s Wager Explained
:Source-c888c9da-2a11-5bb4-a906-aaff39fd4940 a :Video ;
	:name "Pascal’s Wager Explained" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT33M4S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-12-10"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-12-10T05:00:00-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-tLlqtoqMDI"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Pascal’s Wager is one of philosophy’s most famous arguments—promising infinite happiness for a finite cost. In this video, we break down the original argument from Pascal’s Pensées, examine the act-state table, look at the classic objections (many gods, belief vs evidence, mixed strategies, etc.), and explore why the wager ultimately fails as a proof of God’s existence.

But we also look at why Pascal’s Wager succeeds as a tool for thinking about decision theory, belief, probabilities, and high-stakes reasoning.""" ;
	:cites :Source-f8b3a730-2cf1-57fc-9d96-20fcf65c2070 ,	# Pascal's Pensées (Project Gutenberg)
		:Source-4e1bf5f7-031b-50d4-81e1-1845c9029ef1 ;	# Pascal's Wager (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)
.

# fAUV2atmG0M · Do LLMs Actually Understand the World? (Spoiler: No.)
:Source-54e7d9cd-c58e-5499-94ff-1bca7f38618a a :Video ;
	:name "Do LLMs Actually Understand the World? (Spoiler: No.)" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT19M37S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-12-11"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-12-11T05:01:23-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fAUV2atmG0M"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Do large language models actually understand the world, or is that just a convenient myth? In this video, I break down an argument made by Roko Mijic on Liron Shapira's Doom Debates channel.

The argument is a great launching point to think about what models are and whether some sort of transitivity holds. In the end, I conclude that:
Do LLMs have models of the world? No.
But they are proxies for facts about the world insofar as trying to copy the language of their training data would resemble the world.
True claims from LLMs are not mere accidents. But they are more of a happy byproduct than deliberate, logical implications of a world model.""" ;
	:cites :Source-3f514ab0-f4ed-5cf6-a7f3-f7af5bf89120 ;	# Alignment is EASY and Roko's Basilisk is GOOD?! AI Doom Debate with Roko Mijic
.

# vPBYRVieyeY · The Danger of Changing Levels: Fallacies of Composition and Division Explained
:Source-11921ec9-faa2-5fec-937b-fe5d6efdb70a a :Video ;
	:name "The Danger of Changing Levels: Fallacies of Composition and Division Explained" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT16M3S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-12-16"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-12-16T15:36:25-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2893b7ee-72eb-5af3-818f-2005735c8dff ;	# Fallacies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vPBYRVieyeY"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Two Fallacies for the Price of One: Composition and Division!

It's tempting to think that what's true of the parts must be true of the whole, and vice-versa. But that path is fraught with logical peril, often leading to comically terrible results!In this video, we break down the twin fallacies of composition and division, explore the logical structure of both good and bad arguments that 'change levels,' and show you the key question you MUST ask before making such a move.

By the end of this video, you will know:
- What the Fallacy of Composition is (Part to Whole).
- What the Fallacy of Division is (Whole to Part).
- Why \"The car weighs two tons, so the seat weighs two tons\" is wrong.
- Why \"I own this shirt, so I own this button\" is right.
- The crucial test: Is the property preserved under composition/decomposition?

We also touch upon related concepts like Simpson's Paradox, Ecological Fallacies, and the Wisdom of Crowds.

Be skeptical when going from one level to another—always make your premises explicit!""" ;
.

# -ibuWx61LPs · Doomerism Explained: The Case AI Will End Humans
:Source-8fb8e19b-13e9-5425-86f1-ea69f6660c24 a :Video ;
	:name "Doomerism Explained: The Case AI Will End Humans" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT27M12S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-12-18"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-12-18T05:00:01-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-ibuWx61LPs"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """I have expressed a lot of skepticism about AI \"Doomers\" on this channel—but am I being too dismissive? To really test my view, I decided to put on the doomer worldview and \"steelman\" their argument.

In this video, I give my level best attempt at arguing that AI poses a serious existential risk to humanity. I move past the skepticism to formulate and defend the core doomer argument:

1. AI will likely be strictly more powerful than humans.
2. AI will likely be misaligned with humans.
3. We cannot escape the misalignment.
4. If (1), (2), and, (3), then humans face an existential risk from AI.
5. So, humans face an existential risk from AI.

From the \"Guiding Principle of Irrelevance\" to the history of \"Post-Human\" Chess AI, I explore why some believe we are heading toward a catastrophe that we cannot negotiate our way out of.

Stick around for the end of the video where I break character to share my actual takeaway. Did building this argument change my mind, or am I more confident in my anti-doomer stance than ever?""" ;
	:cites :Source-63c6374f-ab39-58f2-92bc-1ce0fc9e91d8 ;	# The Alignment Problem Explained
.

# ktT6nwfVXCk · Ethics Explained: Applied, Normative, and Metaethics in 15 Minutes
:Source-2859ec61-0569-56d9-8604-f312f9312698 a :Video ;
	:name "Ethics Explained: Applied, Normative, and Metaethics in 15 Minutes" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT16M33S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2025-12-22"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2025-12-22T14:55:37-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ktT6nwfVXCk"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Welcome to Ontology Explained! In this video, I walk through the foundations of ethics and the three major branches philosophers use to understand moral questions:

- Applied Ethics – real-world moral issues
- Normative Ethics – theories that explain what makes actions right or wrong
- Metaethics – big-picture questions about what morality is

We’ll talk about Kant’s categorical vs hypothetical imperatives, how philosophers classify actions (obligatory, permissible, forbidden, supererogatory, and more), and how these levels of ethics work together—especially when thinking about AI and technology.

This video sets the groundwork for future discussions on AI ethics, ontology building, and moral reasoning.

What ethical questions about AI or morality do you want answered next?""" ;
	:cites :Source-7cdc62ea-449d-568e-ae99-3c56f66f910c ;	# Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals
.

