# L1zXzSSjTao · Geoffrey Hinton is Wrong About AI Understanding
:Source-8dd25b83-4922-554f-be03-fe34ef9152a0 a :Video ;
	:name "Geoffrey Hinton is Wrong About AI Understanding" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT23M29S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-01-12"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-01-12T05:00:46-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L1zXzSSjTao"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Do AI models actually \"get it,\" or are they just guessing the next word?

In this video, we dive into the philosophical and technical debate over whether Large Language Models (LLMs) truly possess understanding. While many see AI as a sophisticated statistical tool, \"Godfather of AI\" Geoffrey Hinton argues that the internal structures these models build constitute genuine understanding.

In this video, we explore:
- Defining Understanding: What does it mean for a human to understand a task vs. a machine?
- The Reality Gap: Why being trained on text alone might mean LLMs lack a \"grasp\" of the physical world.
- The Hinton Perspective: An analysis of Hinton's claim that internal data structures are the definition of understanding.
- The Rebuttal: Why Hinton’s definition might be missing the vital connection between mind and reality.

Is understanding a structural phenomenon, or does it require a pulse and a place in the real world? Let’s get into it.""" ;
	:cites :Source-103b43e7-14dc-569e-bec6-eeb6a9ec84fd ,	# What Is Understanding? – Geoffrey Hinton | IASEAI 2025
		:Source-54e7d9cd-c58e-5499-94ff-1bca7f38618a ;	# Do LLMs Actually Understand the World? (Spoiler: No.)
.

# KyU601o_o4c · Situating Major Ethical Theories (And Applications for AI!)
:Source-bb0166c9-7694-59a2-a2d6-1bbb3e464288 a :Video ;
	:name "Situating Major Ethical Theories (And Applications for AI!)" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT17M35S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-01-14"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-01-14T05:30:20-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-314c24fd-2173-59f9-bebf-6ab5cf73332b ;	# Ethics Explained
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KyU601o_o4c"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """How do we decide if an action is \"good\" or \"bad\"? Whether you're judging a friend's white lie or a Silicon Valley giant's latest algorithm, you're using a normative ethical framework—even if you don't know it.

In this video, we break down the major schools of ethical thought and see how they apply to the hottest topic in tech: Artificial Intelligence.

In this video, you’ll learn:
- Consequentialism: Why the outcome is all that matters.
- Deontology: Why some rules can never be broken (and what Kant has to do with it).
- Virtue Ethics: Why we should ask \"What would a good person do?\"
- The AI Problem: Since AI can’t actually \"act\" with intent, how do we apply these 2,000-year-old theories to modern code?

We also dive into Asimov’s Laws of Robotics and how different frameworks (like Contractualism and Divine Command Theory) change the way we build and regulate technology.

Is it the rules we give them, or the data we feed them?""" ;
.

