	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sRqatx5xs_o"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """I got a great viewer question on my ontology stitching video. I answer the question, hopefully giving some insight along the way.

Here's the question from Faiza:

\"If we have 2 ontologies with same classes (some ontologies may have additional classes/concepts but overall the concepts are same in all the ontologies), these ontologies may have instances that are similar semantically but written differently. For example, Diabetes_Mellitus in Ontology A and Diabetes in Ontology B. How can we compare these two instances and maintain the aliases? Is it better to create an upper ontology, or should we merge these graphs? And what if we have to trace individual instances back to the original ontology they came from?\"""" ;
	:cites :Source-b40d178e-9bcc-500b-b8b7-9db9678cac0c ;	# Ontology Stitching: How to Align/Merge Enterprise Knowledge Graphs (A Practical Guide)
.

# oa9OWPZwba4 · Epistemology Explained: Knowledge from Plato to AI
:Source-bf681728-9b7c-59bf-b550-7def77fc42ef a :Video ;
	:name "Epistemology Explained: Knowledge from Plato to AI" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT16M40S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-01-27"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-01-27T13:26:00-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oa9OWPZwba4"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """What is epistemology, really?

In this video, I introduce epistemology—the branch of philosophy concerned with knowledge, understanding, and rational belief. Starting from its Greek roots (epistēmē and logos), I explain both the standard definition (the study of knowledge) and a broader way of thinking about it as the study of cognitive success.

We walk through a simple, Feldman-inspired approach:
we know some things, that knowledge seems to come from sources like experience, testimony, and reasoning, and philosophical theories try to explain how that works—and where it might break down. Along the way, we touch on skepticism, error theory, and why humans might know less than we think.

I also introduce Plato’s classic Justified True Belief account of knowledge, with clear examples of what happens when belief, truth, or justification is missing—and why philosophers think that story might be incomplete.

Finally, I sketch where the conversation goes next: Gettier cases, Bayesian epistemology, peer disagreement, contextualism, and the growing connection between epistemology and AI. Can AI be rational? Can AI be part of our justified beliefs? And what would a virtue-based epistemology say about using AI well?

If you’re curious about how knowledge works—whether human or artificial—this is the place to start.""" ;
	:cites :Source-4d0c7f39-6bc8-5086-b434-5bbc1704a1d6 ,	# Logic Explained: The Philosophy of Arguments
		:Source-1af00431-92d5-5da1-8a96-18d7da638b25 ,	# Epistemology (Richard Feldman)
		:Source-327b1cd6-12f3-5563-8290-470955caf6f5 ;	# Epistemology (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)
.

# XsaYPcHrL8Y · AI: The Perfect Scapegoat for Corporate Layoffs
:Source-2d928444-c57e-5089-812e-6249a2d111ab a :Video ;
	:name "AI: The Perfect Scapegoat for Corporate Layoffs" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT13M22S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-01-29"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-01-29T16:41:41-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XsaYPcHrL8Y"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Amazon recently laid off another 16,000 employees, ostensibly due to AI. In this video, I discuss AI as an explanation. While AI is an important factor in how our economy and companies are functioning, I argue that AI is more of a convenient scapegoat than a direct cause in this case.

I conclude by thinking a bit on the safety of ontology jobs. And then I discuss the ethical obligations of companies in these situations.""" ;
.

# _mPjIOJUyHk · What Chess Engines Teach Us About AI and Human Knowledge
:Source-7c04975e-0745-55f0-8a56-5dfa4af2c34a a :Video ;
	:name "What Chess Engines Teach Us About AI and Human Knowledge" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT14M19S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-02-03"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-02-03T10:32:00-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mPjIOJUyHk"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Fabiano Caruana recently pointed out something fascinating about modern chess: today’s elite 14-year-olds aren’t just talented — they’re armed with engines.

With Stockfish, they can instantly tell whether an opening is sound, dubious, or outright losing. What used to take years of painful trial-and-error now takes minutes. When Fabi was growing up, even top juniors would simply get worse positions out of the opening against elite players because they lacked theoretical knowledge. Closing that gap used to take until 18 or later. Now it happens much earlier.

So what changed? Not just skill — tools.

In this video, I use Caruana’s example to think about a philosophical question:
If epistemology is about cognitive success, what role should AI play in a virtuous thinker’s life?

Should we treat AI like chess players treat engines — as training partners and error detectors?
Or does outsourcing judgment risk weakening our intellectual virtues?

I argue that AI likely has a legitimate role in virtuous cognition… but the long-term effects of cognitive offloading are still an open question.

Chess, engines, and virtue epistemology collide.""" ;
	:cites :Source-f7de46f9-eef4-51b1-8751-f67c001923c1 ;	# Gukesh And The Blunder That Shocked The Chess World, The Biggest Chess Prodigy Is Here
.

# jv7jv_gMBKc · Can AI Think in a Private Language? Wittgenstein vs. GPT-6
:Source-349bbf0c-aca2-542b-ae19-ec11b8158043 a :Video ;
	:name "Can AI Think in a Private Language? Wittgenstein vs. GPT-6" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT21M42S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-02-04"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-02-04T08:01:11-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jv7jv_gMBKc"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """People are worried that future AI models might “think in their own secret language.” Sam Altman has even warned that increasingly powerful systems could behave in ways we don’t fully understand. But is this really a new problem—or just an old philosophical one in disguise?

In this video, I take a fast tour through some classic debates in philosophy of language and epistemology—empiricism vs. rationalism, meaning and reference, Wittgenstein’s language games, the private language argument, and Quine’s indeterminacy of translation—to ask a simple question: what would a “secret language” even be?

If meaning is use, then language is fundamentally public and rule-governed. That makes a truly private language—human or AI—hard to even make sense of. And if “secret” just means “hard to interpret,” then humans are already like that.

So maybe the scary idea isn’t that AI is becoming alien. Maybe it’s that language was never as transparent as we thought—not even in our own heads.

Along the way: Wittgenstein, Quine, Kripke’s “quus,” LLMs, the Turing Test, and what it really means to understand something.""" ;
	:cites :Source-fc7fe82e-098f-527c-9d90-41ad3d8f357b ,	# GPT-6's secret language and AI safety (Medium, DevBoostLab)
		:Source-c3a561cc-f0e3-5cf7-853b-760b8d46f88c ,	# Word and Object
		:Source-ecddd71e-868a-57bc-859b-aa2650509910 ;	# Philosophical Investigations
.

# K4G2dvFGIDU · Roko's Basilisk Explained (And Debunked!)
:Source-1485447d-649c-532d-bd15-db49e9da09e3 a :Video ;
	:name "Roko's Basilisk Explained (And Debunked!)" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT28M19S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-02-06"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-02-06T05:00:40-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K4G2dvFGIDU"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Roko’s Basilisk is one of the internet’s strangest philosophical horror stories: a deleted LessWrong post, whispers of “information hazards,” Elon Musk and Grimes lore, and the claim that a future AI might punish you for not helping to create it.

But what is the argument actually supposed to be?

In this episode of Ontology Explained, we slow everything down and go back to the primary sources. I walk through:

- What Roko actually argued
- Why the rationalist community freaked out
- How it mirrors Pascal’s Wager
- And why the reasoning collapses under basic philosophical scrutiny

Along the way we separate myth from reality, critique the expected-value math, and look at what the original post was really trying (and mostly failing) to say.

Spoiler: it’s less “AI apocalypse” and more “confused blog post plus bad decision theory.”""" ;
	:cites :Source-7397576e-2ebd-5804-91d8-e85789d8cf30 ,	# Newcomb’s Problem Explained: One Box Or Two?
		:Source-c888c9da-2a11-5bb4-a906-aaff39fd4940 ,	# Pascal’s Wager Explained
		:Source-4859dbb3-69a8-5a6c-9475-5980a108a12a ,	# BLIT (short story) (Wikipedia)
		:Source-399c9e98-4b8e-5293-ae7a-3dd2a0549869 ,	# BLIT (David Langford)
		:Source-2c7c48e1-4398-5659-ab74-a8e8da208c50 ,	# Roko's original LessWrong post (archived)
		:Source-6aafd0ba-0790-5ab5-a31f-5de658813969 ,	# A few misconceptions surrounding Roko's basilisk (LessWrong)
		:Source-30be83ce-4804-5e1e-b738-ee4a835de40a ,	# Public Choice and the Altruist's Burden (LessWrong)
		:Source-3f514ab0-f4ed-5cf6-a7f3-f7af5bf89120 ,	# Alignment is EASY and Roko's Basilisk is GOOD?! AI Doom Debate with Roko Mijic
		:Source-defb1d92-98ad-5797-bec2-731bc25e3b70 ,	# Coherent Extrapolated Volition (LessWrong)
		:Source-fc4def1f-052a-5166-9255-32576a10455d ;	# Behind the Bastards: Part One: The Zizians
.

# 7GfQve21mCI · The Ship of Theseus Explained and Why It Matters for Data Modeling
:Source-885cbdbf-1fd3-5bc1-8c9c-c76fc8dacca0 a :Video ;
	:name "The Ship of Theseus Explained and Why It Matters for Data Modeling" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT22M1S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-02-18"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-02-18T06:00:17-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7GfQve21mCI"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """I had to replace my car’s transmission… but somehow it’s still the same car.
Is it really?

In this episode we dive into one of the oldest and strangest puzzles in metaphysics: the Ship of Theseus. Starting with the legend of Theseus, recorded by Plutarch and later expanded by Thomas Hobbes, we ask a deceptively simple question: if you replace something piece by piece, is it still the same thing?

We explore numerical versus qualitative identity, Leibniz’s Law, essential versus accidental properties, and persistence conditions. Along the way we see why every metaphysical theory sounds completely reasonable at first and then totally crazy once you follow it through.

But this is not just ivory tower philosophy. These same problems show up in ontology engineering, data modeling, defining what counts as a vehicle, deciding whether a VIN determines identity, and resolving messy real world data. Even sports franchises raise identity puzzles, like whether the Seattle SuperSonics are the same team as the Oklahoma City Thunder.

Every schema quietly commits you to a metaphysics. What kinds of things exist? Which properties are essential? When does something stop being the same object?

If you build databases, knowledge graphs, or ontologies, you are already doing metaphysics whether you realize it or not.

By the end, you will have practical philosophical tools to think more clearly about identity, persistence, and entity resolution in your own systems.""" ;
.

# T0Y9rw0c9j0 · An Ontologist Explains Themselves: Alex Shifrin
:Source-4f0413bf-8cca-541e-9b48-2b9021a7b9e0 a :Video ;
	:name "An Ontologist Explains Themselves: Alex Shifrin" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT56M57S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-02-20"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-02-20T17:23:47-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-b104a790-0284-52e2-af18-d2c83dfdfce3 ;	# The Ontology Explained Podcast
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T0Y9rw0c9j0"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In this episode I sit down with Alex Shifrin to talk about what it actually means to be an ontologist and how ontology work shows up in the real world. Alex shares his path through industry roles at Moody's, Gro Intelligence, and Siemens, and explains how semantic modeling and knowledge graphs create practical value inside large organizations.

We break down what an ontologist does in plain English, how someone gets into the field, and whether it is a career worth pursuing today. We also talk candidly about the job market, how to actually find work, and why experienced practitioners tend to reuse and extend existing ontologies instead of building everything from scratch.

Along the way we cover whether corn is a class, the tradeoffs between solo and team based modeling, and the early warning signs that a knowledge graph project will succeed or fail. If you care about knowledge graphs, semantic web, data modeling, or building systems that make messy data interoperable, this conversation is packed with practical insight.""" ;
.

# PcsJPrQUoJ4 · Heather Hedden: Are Taxonomists Ontologists (or Vice-Versa)?
:Source-1890597f-10cd-510c-8f7d-b26b077ea69c a :Video ;
	:name "Heather Hedden: Are Taxonomists Ontologists (or Vice-Versa)?" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT48M31S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-03-03"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-03-03T14:14:28-08:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-b104a790-0284-52e2-af18-d2c83dfdfce3 ;	# The Ontology Explained Podcast
