# 4pEIMvxgCik · Knowledge Engineers Rock: Keith Corbett
:Source-1441bac1-3002-5020-a7aa-bf13102c4d2d a :Video ;
	:name "Knowledge Engineers Rock: Keith Corbett" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT57M10S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-04-01"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-04-01T22:27:25-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-b104a790-0284-52e2-af18-d2c83dfdfce3 ;	# The Ontology Explained Podcast
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4pEIMvxgCik"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In this episode, I sit down with Keith Corbett to talk about the past, present, and future of AI—from the early days of GOFAI to today’s knowledge graph–driven systems.

Keith shares his journey from working at Lisp Machines Inc and Interleaf, where he supported customers building rule-based systems and early chatbots, through the AI Winter, and into the modern era of semantic technologies. Since 2015, he’s focused on OWL/RDF, knowledge graphs, and semantic layers, helping organizations build more structured, meaningful AI systems.

We also dive into what it means to become an ontologist, how to think about ontology as a discipline, and Keith’s experience leading cohorts in the KGC Learning Program. If you're interested in knowledge graphs, AI architecture, or breaking into ontology work, this conversation is packed with insight.

Topics include:
- The evolution from GOFAI to modern AI
- Knowledge representation and semantic layers
- What ontologists actually do
- Teaching and learning in the KGC program
- Building AI systems grounded in knowledge graphs""" ;
	:cites :Source-963f10a9-3d27-5f83-9d8c-d8a596592278 ;	# The Knowledge Graph Conference
.

# qZquY_r5O10 · My Experience at KGC 2026 | AI, Ontologies, and the “Year of Context”
:Source-369f4336-10f6-5cfc-acb3-92b6ec8df64c a :Video ;
	:name "My Experience at KGC 2026 | AI, Ontologies, and the “Year of Context”" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT18M56S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-05-15"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-05-15T17:26:54-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qZquY_r5O10"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Just got back from KGC 2026 (Knowledge Graph Conference), and I wanted to share some thoughts, highlights, concerns, and big-picture takeaways from the event.

In this video I cover:

- Why I haven’t uploaded many videos lately
- Why uploads should return to a regular pace
- Upcoming podcasts and interviews

Then we dive into KGC 2026 itself:
Founded in 2019 by Thomas Deely and Francois Scharffe, KGC has quickly become one of the central conferences for the knowledge graph, ontology, and semantic technology world.

Big takeaways from the conference:

1. Everyone is talking about LLMs + semantics as the bridge between enterprise data and AI.
2. The market is HOT. Gartner is now describing semantic technologies and knowledge graphs as “critical infrastructure,” alongside data platforms and cybersecurity.
3. There’s a real sense that NOW is the time to get involved.

Conference highlights:

- Meeting viewers and members of the community in person
- Reconnecting with friends and colleagues like Alex Shifrin, Maru, and Heather
- Great conversations with new people like Eloragh
- Ora Lassila and Jim Hendler’s Wednesday keynote
- “Understanding 20th-Century Science Patronage through Knowledge Graphs”
- “RDF 1.2 Status Report”
- “The Context Layer: Knowledge Graphs' Second Act” from Atlan

I also talk about some worries:

- Confusion around what “semantics” actually means
- The gap between vision and tangible implementation
- Whether KGC is fully representative of the broader ontology community

Overall though, the energy was strong and optimistic.

If you’re interested in ontologies, semantic web tech, knowledge graphs, RDF, OWL, AI, or linked data, this community is still relatively small and surprisingly accessible.""" ;
	:cites :Source-3fb9c9b8-bb08-5af2-85ef-ded52faf784b ,	# The Semantic Web (Scientific American, May 2001)
		:Source-dff08208-c8d8-5fb3-b008-0583e1410087 ;	# The Knowledge Graph Conference (YouTube channel)
.

