# KLiePXY2XM4 · What Ontology Actually Is | Casey Reacts
:Source-c83b9e65-40df-52ce-b1ce-034d57976a0d a :Video ;
	:name "What Ontology Actually Is | Casey Reacts" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT24M46S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-07-24"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-07-24T17:34:57-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-1c3f9f59-deb4-5fe5-94fa-a85120e597fc ;	# Intro to Ontology
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KLiePXY2XM4"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """I read Jawahar Talluri's article, \"What an Ontology Actually Is,\" and I think it's a solid introduction to why ontologies matter—but I also think it oversimplifies what an ontology actually is.

In this response, I discuss where I agree with the article, where I think its definition falls short, and why I prefer to think of an ontology in three parts:

1. A language — a vocabulary for talking about a domain.
2. A model of reality — a representation of the things that exist and how they relate.
3. A mathematical structure — a graph that formally connects those things.

I also discuss why I think instance data belongs within the ontology, why I'm not convinced that constraints are a defining component of ontology itself, and why the article's framing seems primarily designed around using ontologies as infrastructure for generative AI and enterprise knowledge graphs.

The article makes a good case for ontologies as infrastructure. But I think there's a much broader story to tell about what ontologies are, what they can represent, and why they matter beyond GenAI.""" ;
	:cites :Source-24c68d1b-aae3-58eb-af7e-877725f4b482 ;	# What an Ontology Actually Is: The Semantic Layer, in Plain Terms, and Why It Is Infrastructure
.

# gr4_UxIoE9Y · If You Give a Mouse a Cookie... The Slippery Slope Fallacy
:Source-587c19ab-5c63-51b3-86b5-7de55910ad6f a :Video ;
	:name "If You Give a Mouse a Cookie... The Slippery Slope Fallacy" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT14M17S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-07-27"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-07-27T11:51:38-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2893b7ee-72eb-5af3-818f-2005735c8dff ;	# Fallacies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gr4_UxIoE9Y"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """What happens if you give a mouse a cookie?

Well, he's going to want a glass of milk. And before you know it, you're doing mouse haircuts, mouse naps, and mouse refrigerator art.

Is this just a classic children's story—or a cautionary tale about the slippery slope fallacy?

In this video, we take a closer look at the slippery slope fallacy: what it is, why it's often a bad argument, and why some slopes really are slippery.

We'll look at:

- The basic structure of a slippery slope argument
- Classic examples from moral panics and political debates
- Why slippery slope arguments often fail
- How probabilities combine across a chain of events
- Cases where slippery slopes actually can be legitimate
- The tricky case of prediction markets and betting on harmful events
- Slippery slope arguments about AI, including both AI doomer and AI \"zoomer\" perspectives
- Whether the future of AI is a terrifying descent—or a waterslide toward utopia

A slippery slope argument claims that a relatively simple action will eventually lead to disastrous consequences. But the fact that the consequences would be terrible doesn't establish that the causal chain leading to them is inevitable—or even likely.

The key question is: What actually connects each step of the slope?

If the causal mechanism is vague, unsupported, or relies primarily on the severity of the final outcome to scare us away from the first step, we may be dealing with a fallacy.

But sometimes slopes really are slippery. Addiction, security vulnerabilities, and certain behavioral patterns can create genuine chains of consequences. The challenge is figuring out which is which.

Which will give me fame. Which I can parlay into political office. And then, when I rule the world, I will bring about a utopia for all.""" ;
	:cites :Source-23c7de76-c833-5677-9fc1-1199d7cf1ba1 ;	# Matt Foley: Van Down By The River - SNL
.

# GEdhpq3Ri_o · An Honest Breakdown of Eliezer Yudkowsky's AI Doom Book
:Source-068212fd-bd9b-569d-94d7-4bb8b5f4ec20 a :Video ;
	:name "An Honest Breakdown of Eliezer Yudkowsky's AI Doom Book" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT19M56S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-07-29"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-07-29T15:41:02-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2eeaeeb7-7a52-5801-98e2-ae162f1789ba ;	# If Anyone Analyzes It... Everyone Dies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GEdhpq3Ri_o"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In this first episode of my walkthrough of If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies by Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares, I lay out the goals and methodology for the series before diving into the book's Introduction: Hard Calls and Easy Calls.

This isn't a reaction video or a hit piece. It's an attempt to reconstruct the strongest version of the authors' arguments and evaluate them as charitably and rigorously as possible.

I've spent years reading Yudkowsky's work and following discussions around AI alignment and existential risk. While I'm deeply skeptical of many of the book's arguments, I also believe that serious claims deserve serious engagement. If the authors are right, the stakes couldn't be much higher. If they're wrong, it's important to understand exactly where the arguments fail.

In this episode, we examine:

- How the Introduction establishes the stakes of the AI alignment debate.
- The book's definitions of Artificial Superintelligence (ASI) and the Alignment Problem.
- The analogies and framing devices used to shape the reader's understanding of the problem.
- Which claims are relatively uncontroversial—and which are already asking us to make significant philosophical leaps.

My goal throughout this series is to be thorough, charitable, and honest. I'll reconstruct the arguments as strongly as I can, but I won't hesitate to point out where I think they succeed or fail.

Next episode: Chapter 1 – Humanity's Special Power.""" ;
	:cites :Source-033894ad-9bc4-547f-a531-54bb73f9da12 ;	# If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies
.

# pNwHu5ecsmI · I Asked ChatGPT to Build an Ontology. Here's What Happened.
:Source-2dbc6333-62d6-5bd4-9ea8-62d5f052373d a :Video ;
	:name "I Asked ChatGPT to Build an Ontology. Here's What Happened." ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT41M58S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-07-31"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-07-31T15:46:46-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pNwHu5ecsmI"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Can ChatGPT actually make a good ontology?

In this video, I put an LLM to the test. I gave ChatGPT a Zillow listing for a residential property and asked it to generate a Turtle (TTL) representation of the data. Then I evaluated the resulting model—not just asking whether the TTL worked, but whether the underlying ontology was actually any good.

The result? The TTL was functional. ChatGPT could produce a usable RDF representation of the property. But when it came to the modeling itself, the results were much less impressive. The distinctions between concepts weren't always well thought out, and the ontology was underdeveloped, if not just making false claims.

My takeaway is that LLMs may be useful for generating a quick first draft of an ontology—especially as a starting point or a foil for human analysis. But if you're building a model that needs to be useful and maintainable over the long term, you probably shouldn't rely on an LLM to do the ontology engineering for you.

In this video, I walk through the generated model and evaluate where it works, where it falls short, and what this tells us about using LLMs for ontology development.

Can LLMs make ontologies? Yes.

Can they make good ontologies? That's a much more interesting question. (Probably not.)""" ;
.

# zEQs6vjhxA4 · You Shouldn't Let ChatGPT Write Your Emails
:Source-c2619733-193c-513b-9fbb-1b5d28a491aa a :Video ;
	:name "You Shouldn't Let ChatGPT Write Your Emails" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT16M44S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-08-04"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-08-04T16:47:54-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zEQs6vjhxA4"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Is it morally wrong to send emails that are mostly written by AI?

In this episode, I argue that the answer is often yes.

Rather than debating whether large language models are \"good\" or \"bad\" in general, I focus on a much narrower claim: sending emails that are primarily generated by an LLM is frequently a moral mistake.

I develop two arguments:

- It can involve a kind of deception by presenting AI-generated words as your own communication.
- It can be selfish and anti-relational by offloading the effort of communication while expecting others to engage with the result.

Along the way I discuss changing social norms around AI, whether widespread AI use changes the ethics, and why authenticity still matters even in an age of increasingly capable language models.

I also explain where I think AI use is perfectly appropriate—for editing, brainstorming, improving clarity, generating metadata, standardizing formats, and other cases where it genuinely serves the reader rather than replacing the human author.""" ;
	:cites :Source-d93609a4-5541-599f-b871-4998663a2735 ;	# European Commission: safer and more transparent AI (Aug 2, 2026)
.

# _vkregEIA-4 · The Case for Superintelligence Is Surprisingly Thin | If Anyone Builds It... Chapter 1
:Source-0b6812d1-04ae-5c0d-9b22-b44b5a81bf46 a :Video ;
	:name "The Case for Superintelligence Is Surprisingly Thin | If Anyone Builds It... Chapter 1" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT36M43S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-08-08"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-08-08T09:42:42-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2eeaeeb7-7a52-5801-98e2-ae162f1789ba ;	# If Anyone Analyzes It... Everyone Dies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_vkregEIA-4"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """What makes humans so powerful? According to Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares in If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies, the answer is intelligence—and intelligence is fundamentally about predicting the world and steering it.

In this episode of Ontology Explained, I take a critical look at the book's opening argument.

I agree with the basic idea that human intelligence has played a major role in our extraordinary impact on the planet. But I think the book moves far too quickly over some very difficult questions. What actually makes humans special? What is intelligence? Is prediction really one half of intelligence and steering the other? And why should we be so confident that AI will inevitably surpass human intelligence?

I'll look at the book's brief story about human dominance, its conception of intelligence as prediction and steering, the philosophical alternative of beliefs and desires, and the arguments for why AI will eventually eclipse humanity.

My main concern is not that the book's conclusions are obviously wrong. It's that some of its most important premises are surprisingly underdeveloped. There is little engagement with anthropology, philosophy of mind, or the existing philosophical literature on concepts like belief, desire, and direction of fit.

And that's a problem, because the possibility of superintelligent AI is doing a lot of work in the argument. If ASI is genuinely plausible, then its potential consequences are worth taking very seriously. But we need a stronger argument for why we should believe it's coming.

This is my ongoing series reviewing and critiquing If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies. I'll continue on to Chapter 2—but with some skepticism about the foundations laid in Chapter 1.""" ;
	:cites :Source-068212fd-bd9b-569d-94d7-4bb8b5f4ec20 ,	# An Honest Breakdown of Eliezer Yudkowsky's AI Doom Book
		:Source-033894ad-9bc4-547f-a531-54bb73f9da12 ;	# If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies
.

# Y94_zNPZJ9c · The Most Common Fallacy? | Affirming the Consequent
:Source-17e30c31-fc4e-54f5-a4f6-306171c409c5 a :Video ;
	:name "The Most Common Fallacy? | Affirming the Consequent" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT11M24S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-08-10"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-08-10T09:00:29-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2893b7ee-72eb-5af3-818f-2005735c8dff ;	# Fallacies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y94_zNPZJ9c"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """If it’s raining, the streets are wet.
The streets are wet.
Therefore, it’s raining.

Seems reasonable, right?

Well… not so fast.

In this episode of Ontology Explained, we take a look at affirming the consequent, one of the most common logical fallacies. I’ll explain exactly what the fallacy is, introduce some useful terminology from formal logic, and show why the argument is invalid.

We’ll look at some classic examples—including one involving the Turing Test and AI—and then tackle a more interesting question: when does an argument that looks like affirming the consequent actually give us good reason to believe its conclusion?

That leads us to explanatory reasoning and inference to the best explanation. The key is understanding the difference between a deductively valid argument and an argument that gives us good evidence.

So: if the streets are wet, did it rain? Maybe. But you can't prove it just from that.""" ;
.

# uc6OURMyXgs · Are AIs "Grown" or Built? Analyzing Chapter 2 of If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies
:Source-f343164e-a43d-5523-bfca-35d4e73a7dc9 a :Video ;
	:name "Are AIs \"Grown\" or Built? Analyzing Chapter 2 of If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT44M15S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-08-14"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-08-14T07:00:27-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2eeaeeb7-7a52-5801-98e2-ae162f1789ba ;	# If Anyone Analyzes It... Everyone Dies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uc6OURMyXgs"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Are AI models \"grown\" like crops, or engineered like software? And why are AI safety writers so obsessed with painting LLMs as bizarre, unpredictable alien minds?

In this video, we continue our chapter-by-chapter breakdown of \"If Anyone Builds This, Everyone Dies\" by analyzing Chapter 2. We put their provocative \"Grown, Not Crafted\" analogy to the test, untangle the messy dialectic around LLM autocomplete vs. \"true understanding,\" and address the infamous incident where Bing Chat allegedly threatened a philosophy professor.

While there is a modest, valid point buried here—that modern deep-learning models are too complex for humans to predict in every scenario—the broader claims about AI preferences and \"alien minds\" are woefully under-defended and rely on subtle leaps in logic.""" ;
	:cites :Source-033894ad-9bc4-547f-a531-54bb73f9da12 ,	# If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies
		:Source-5f938086-96b2-547a-833f-f97d4278f1bf ,	# Chauhan & Geiger (2024), paper on OpenReview
		:Source-0884faed-c805-5456-9972-d3d3d7f55d70 ,	# Chauhan & Geiger, NeurIPS 2024 poster
		:Source-fb1e424f-5b3e-5740-8b1a-4147b22c9447 ,	# Agassi on Becker's Tongue Tell
		:Source-b8aea38e-98d9-50dc-92f8-b2a43e54acb7 ,	# ELIZA effect (Wikipedia)
		:Source-6ffe17d7-ddf8-5b87-b36e-d5124ee1a599 ,	# Pareidolia (Wikipedia)
		:Source-e261e102-f486-5d7d-988e-e5e84691a380 ;	# Adam Dunn better than Hall of Famer Tony Gwynn? (Bleacher Report)
.

# AAxm9qbqLrs · Does AI Really Want Anything? | If Anyone Builds It... Chapter 3
:Source-366b3d55-c5bf-53ce-b656-f68461907f10 a :Video ;
	:name "Does AI Really Want Anything? | If Anyone Builds It... Chapter 3" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT28M32S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-08-17"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-08-17T08:00:27-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2eeaeeb7-7a52-5801-98e2-ae162f1789ba ;	# If Anyone Analyzes It... Everyone Dies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAxm9qbqLrs"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Do AI systems actually want things?

In this chapter of Yudkowsky and Soares' If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies, the authors argue that sufficiently capable AIs will begin to behave as though they have preferences and wants. They appeal to evolution, gradient descent, strategic behavior, and game theory to make their case.

I’m not convinced.

In this video, I examine their argument about AI wanting things, starting with a seemingly simple question: What does it mean to want something? Does being shaped to achieve a particular outcome really amount to wanting that outcome? And if it does, do marbles, blankets, sorting algorithms, and paramecia have wants too?

I also look at their examples of AI behavior, including the famous story of o1 \"going hard,\" and ask whether these examples actually provide evidence for desires and mental states—or whether we're simply using metaphorical language to describe sophisticated problem-solving.

Finally, I look at the chapter's discussion of game theory and its broader conclusion: that AI will be difficult to get to behave exactly as we want.

That last point? I agree with it.

But I'm not sure we needed all the talk about AI wanting things to get there.

This is part of my ongoing series examining If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies from the perspective of analytic philosophy, ontology, and AI.""" ;
	:cites :Source-033894ad-9bc4-547f-a531-54bb73f9da12 ;	# If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies
.

# XhF_49e6Cpg · Do LLMs Really Understand? Examining Geoffrey Hinton's Arguments
:Source-ad5d79ad-61cf-5b0c-b944-4985de371ab3 a :Video ;
	:name "Do LLMs Really Understand? Examining Geoffrey Hinton's Arguments" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT28M17S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-08-19"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-08-19T08:00:24-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XhF_49e6Cpg"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Geoffrey Hinton thinks today's AI systems understand us. I'm not so sure.

In a recent interview with Alex Kantrowitz, Hinton gives two arguments for the claim that large language models really do understand. In this video, I take both arguments seriously and ask whether they actually establish what Hinton thinks they establish.

The first argument is roughly: if LLMs can consistently give us the right answers, then they must understand. The second points to an especially interesting phenomenon: when a chatbot gives a wrong answer, gets corrected, and responds by saying \"I misunderstood,\" perhaps the best explanation is that it really did understand.

I think both arguments have some real philosophical structure to them—but I don't think they get us all the way to understanding.

Along the way, I discuss:
- The difference between understanding language and understanding the world
- Why \"getting the right answer\" may not require understanding
- The Chinese Room and the example of calculators
- Why selection bias makes LLM accuracy difficult to assess
- Why saying \"I misunderstood\" isn't necessarily evidence of understanding
- What would actually convince us that an opaque AI system contains a mind

My current view is that LLMs seem to have something like an understanding of language use, but that's not obviously the same thing as understanding the things that language represents.

Hinton may be right. But I think we need better evidence.""" ;
	:cites :Source-674eff03-e701-539d-914c-c8a2a66e9777 ,	# AI Pioneer Geoffrey Hinton: AI Is Conscious, Superintelligence is Coming, And We Should Be Worried
		:Source-8dd25b83-4922-554f-be03-fe34ef9152a0 ,	# Geoffrey Hinton is Wrong About AI Understanding
		:Source-02778b95-9319-5bab-b1d0-cef645d64966 ;	# Nature article on benchmarking
.

# cFBSgW-py3E · Appeal to Ignorance: When “We Don’t Know” Goes Wrong
:Source-ae519123-7fb3-5a3b-9f55-a90df609d538 a :Video ;
	:name "Appeal to Ignorance: When “We Don’t Know” Goes Wrong" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT10M49S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-08-25"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-08-25T08:00:26-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2893b7ee-72eb-5af3-818f-2005735c8dff ;	# Fallacies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cFBSgW-py3E"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """We don't know.

And sometimes, that's all we know.

But does our ignorance give us reason to believe something else?

In this video, I take a look at the logical fallacy known as the appeal to ignorance: the mistake of treating our lack of knowledge about something as evidence for a related conclusion.

We'll look at examples involving reincarnation, evolution, and even AI—and then we'll complicate things. Because while \"absence of evidence is not evidence of absence\" is a useful rule of thumb, there really are cases where failing to find evidence does give us reason to believe something is false.

We'll cover:

- What an appeal to ignorance actually is
- Why these arguments are usually bad reasoning
- How to spot them in the wild
- When absence of evidence really is evidence of absence
- Ockham's Razor and other legitimate uses of epistemic defaults
- Why \"we don't know\" doesn't always mean \"anything goes\"""" ;
	:cites :Source-2eeaeeb7-7a52-5801-98e2-ae162f1789ba ;	# playlist: If Anyone Analyzes It... Everyone Dies
.

# _lCH9FQMWoE · Can We Predict What AI Will Want? | Yudkowsky & Soares Chapter 4 Part 1
:Source-efbc814c-9d01-5ffb-9ad1-583a1c0b118f a :Video ;
	:name "Can We Predict What AI Will Want? | Yudkowsky & Soares Chapter 4 Part 1" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT30M39S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-08-27"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-08-27T08:00:38-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2eeaeeb7-7a52-5801-98e2-ae162f1789ba ;	# If Anyone Analyzes It... Everyone Dies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_lCH9FQMWoE"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """What can the evolution of human desires teach us about the future of AI?

In Chapter 4 of If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies, Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares argue that we shouldn't expect an AI to simply develop the preferences we intended to give it. They draw an analogy between the way human desires emerged through evolution and the way an AI's \"wants\" might emerge through training.

I think there's an important and interesting idea here—but I don't think their argument establishes nearly as much as they want it to.

In Part 1 of my discussion of Chapter 4, I look closely at their analogy between natural selection and AI training. I agree that systems can produce surprising results when they're moved outside the context in which they were developed. But that's a long way from showing that AI will develop preferences that are bizarre, unpredictable, and catastrophically opposed to human interests.

I also argue that Yudkowsky and Soares' analogy becomes confused when they compare evolution and gradient descent, and that a much stronger argument for their general point could have been made using historical examples of technologies that produced radically unexpected consequences.

This is Part 1 of my discussion of Chapter 4. Part 2 takes up their speculative scenarios about what an AI might come to \"want\" and their argument about the Alignment Problem.

Chapter discussed:
If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies, Chapter 4: \"You Don't Get What You Train For\"""" ;
	:cites :Source-033894ad-9bc4-547f-a531-54bb73f9da12 ,	# If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies
		:Source-a8c082eb-f91d-589b-a304-66af29a898e6 ,	# Microwaves and radar (History Cambridge)
		:Source-d47478a8-5b2d-5e03-b786-d0c0db084995 ,	# The history of gunpowder, from elixir of life to the revolution of warfare (History Guild)
		:Source-086d0df7-4dc3-55f9-885c-014f9fadbbad ,	# Thalidomide (Wikipedia)
		:Source-ef10c586-963e-5dad-bb46-a2b5a7d6e475 ;	# Watermelon, arugula, and pine nut salad (Epicurious)
.

# yYpZADx3AI0 · Yudkowsky and Soares Needed an Editor | Chapter 4, Part 2 of If Anyone Builds It
:Source-bc027c27-133d-5ff5-9bf2-23d00a429e18 a :Video ;
	:name "Yudkowsky and Soares Needed an Editor | Chapter 4, Part 2 of If Anyone Builds It" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT33M23S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-08-28"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-08-28T08:00:02-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2eeaeeb7-7a52-5801-98e2-ae162f1789ba ;	# If Anyone Analyzes It... Everyone Dies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yYpZADx3AI0"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """What if an AI develops preferences that are very different from what we intended?

In Part 2 of my discussion of Chapter 4 of If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies, Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares imagine a series of increasingly strange ways an AI might come to \"want\" things that its creators never intended.

And I have some problems with these scenarios.

The chapter's central point is that we shouldn't assume an AI will simply want whatever we trained it to want. I agree with that. But the authors move from this relatively modest claim to much stronger claims about AI preferences being bizarre, unpredictable, and almost certainly catastrophically misaligned with human interests.

I don't think the arguments they give justify that leap.

In this video, I go through their five scenarios, including the strange \"SolidGoldMagikarp\" case, and ask what these thought experiments actually establish. I argue that the scenarios systematically ignore benign possibilities, make unsupported claims about how likely different outcomes are, and often don't explain how the more extreme outcomes would actually arise.

Then we get to the chapter's final sections: Yudkowsky and Soares explicitly admit that their scenarios aren't predictions and that they don't know whether an LLM-based AI could even develop these sorts of preferences. But they nevertheless conclude that mature AI preferences will be \"vanishingly unlikely\" to align with ours.

That's where I think the argument really breaks down.

The distinction I keep coming back to throughout the video is simple:

AI might surprise us.

That does not by itself show that AI will develop preferences that are catastrophically opposed to human interests.

I finish by looking at their treatment of the Alignment Problem and arguing that the chapter's most important conclusion receives surprisingly little actual argument.

This is Part 2 of my discussion of Chapter 4, \"You Don't Get What You Train For,\" from If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies by Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares.""" ;
	:cites :Source-efbc814c-9d01-5ffb-9ad1-583a1c0b118f ,	# Can We Predict What AI Will Want? | Yudkowsky & Soares Chapter 4 Part 1
		:Source-033894ad-9bc4-547f-a531-54bb73f9da12 ,	# If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies
		:Source-0617e266-abef-5150-b679-7b6369a80c97 ,	# SolidGoldMagikarp (plus, prompt generation) (LessWrong)
		:Source-b5271cac-96dc-57f1-b942-a87fee5d7a2b ;	# TensorFlow 2 feature visualization: visualizing classes (Tim Sainburg)
.

# TZJj9zZ1xto · Will Superintelligent AI Want to Kill Us? | Chapter 5
:Source-bc65fdaf-d16f-5de3-a1d6-bac87a2dfd71 a :Video ;
	:name "Will Superintelligent AI Want to Kill Us? | Chapter 5" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT38M27S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-09-02T07:30:09-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2eeaeeb7-7a52-5801-98e2-ae162f1789ba ;	# If Anyone Analyzes It... Everyone Dies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TZJj9zZ1xto"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """In Chapter 5 of If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies, Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares make their clearest case yet for why a superintelligent AI would want humanity gone.

Their central argument is built around a strange thought experiment: alien birds who desperately care about building \"correct nests\"—nests containing a prime number of stones. The idea is that just as we can't imagine why an alien intelligence would care about our values, an ASI won't care about ours.

But does the analogy actually work?

In this video, I reconstruct the argument of Chapter 5 and examine some of its key assumptions: moral relativism, the idea that an ASI's values would necessarily conflict with ours, instrumental convergence, the \"billionaire argument,\" and the assumption that a sufficiently powerful AI would inevitably dominate and eliminate us.

I also look at one of the chapter's stranger turns: the suggestion that a future without humanity wouldn't be worth having because machines wouldn't create a world filled with \"wonder and joy and humor and love.\"

By the end, I think Chapter 5 contains the book's most coherent argument so far—but I'm still not convinced they've met the burden of proof.""" ;
	:cites :Source-033894ad-9bc4-547f-a531-54bb73f9da12 ,	# If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies
		:Source-6bc291be-21b4-5079-b3c9-720b477af0f3 ;	# A Darwinian Dilemma for Realist Theories of Value
.

# lCfIt6HKrdc · Tu Quoque Explained: The Fallacy of Hypocrisy
:Source-0c513b4d-67b2-5bb1-a4b8-f93a38860afd a :Video ;
	:name "Tu Quoque Explained: The Fallacy of Hypocrisy" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT15M14S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-09-04T15:30:14-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2893b7ee-72eb-5af3-818f-2005735c8dff ;	# Fallacies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lCfIt6HKrdc"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """There's nothing quite as satisfying as catching someone in a contradiction. But does proving that someone is a hypocrite actually show that they're wrong?

In this video, we take a close look at the tu quoque fallacy—Latin for \"you too\"—and ask when accusations of hypocrisy are bad reasoning and when they actually matter.

We'll cover:

- What the tu quoque fallacy is
- How it relates to ad hominem and red herring fallacies
- Why someone's hypocrisy usually doesn't make their argument false
- Why proving hypocrisy is often harder than it looks
- Examples from politics, including arguments about abortion and presidential age
- When hypocrisy is relevant to an argument
- Why Jesus' teaching about hypocrisy doesn't commit the tu quoque fallacy

The basic lesson: don't reject an argument merely because the person making it doesn't practice what they preach. But that doesn't mean hypocrisy is never relevant. Sometimes it reveals an inconsistency in someone's worldview or gives us evidence about their reliability.

And, of course, there's one final lesson: don't be a hypocrite about hypocrisy.""" ;
.

# FgBWKp2G7SY · Can Humanity Conquer a Rogue ASI? | Chapter 6
:Source-176f2df7-1884-5088-bd02-40adddf5aa3b a :Video ;
	:name "Can Humanity Conquer a Rogue ASI? | Chapter 6" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT26M40S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-09-14"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-09-14T10:53:09-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2eeaeeb7-7a52-5801-98e2-ae162f1789ba ;	# If Anyone Analyzes It... Everyone Dies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FgBWKp2G7SY"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Would humans really have no chance against a superintelligent AI?

In Chapter 6 of If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies, Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares argue that if we end up in conflict with a superintelligent AI, we should expect to lose. Their central analogy is the Aztecs facing the Spanish: the technological gap was so large that the Aztecs couldn't even anticipate the ways their opponents could defeat them.

There's something to this argument. A sufficiently advanced AI might have capabilities that we simply can't foresee.

But I think the Aztec analogy is much less convincing than Yudkowsky and Soares think it is.

So in this video, I look at their argument, dig into what actually happened during the Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire, and ask whether technological superiority really explains the outcome. The answer is considerably more complicated than \"the Spanish were smarter and had better technology.\"

And that raises a broader philosophical problem with the argument: if we're allowed to imagine an arbitrarily capable AI using methods we can't anticipate, why can't we imagine humans finding some way to stop it?

Would we lose?

Maybe. But I'm not convinced that our ability to imagine hypothetical AI victories tells us very much about what would actually happen.

This is Chapter 6 of my ongoing series on If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies by Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares.""" ;
	:cites :Source-033894ad-9bc4-547f-a531-54bb73f9da12 ,	# If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies
		:Source-412ad6c6-6f51-57ca-afb4-56a168a2d3fb ,	# FALL of the Aztecs: How 400 Spaniards Toppled an Empire | Animated History
		:Source-3a431157-be3c-57af-9705-4ddf74b3b70a ;	# The Conquest of the Aztec Empire (ThoughtCo)
.

# sf948U7hPcY · Can the Liar Paradox Prove AI Can't Be Controlled?
:Source-73ce4acf-cd1d-5b1c-b453-b9922c022dc4 a :Video ;
	:name "Can the Liar Paradox Prove AI Can't Be Controlled?" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT25M8S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-09-23"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-09-23T13:30:29-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sf948U7hPcY"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """Can the Liar Paradox show that AI cannot be controlled?

In this video, I examine an argument from AI safety researcher Dr. Roman Yampolskiy, who appeals to the classic Liar Paradox as part of an argument for the uncontrollability of artificial intelligence.

The basic idea is intriguing: if we explicitly tell an AI to \"disobey,\" what happens? Yampolskiy suggests that this creates a kind of paradox that reveals a fundamental problem with explicit AI control.

But does it?

To find out, we'll take a detour into the philosophy of the Liar Paradox. What exactly makes the Liar paradoxical? What role do principles like the Law of Excluded Middle, the T-schema, and explosion play? And do any of these features carry over when we're dealing with an imperative like \"Disobey!\" rather than a declarative sentence?

I'll argue that they don't—and that the Liar Paradox doesn't provide the argument Yampolskiy needs. That doesn't settle the larger question of whether AI can ultimately be controlled, but it does give us reason to reject this particular argument.

We'll also look at the logic of imperatives, the distinction between satisfiable and unsatisfiable commands, and the familiar puzzle of whether an omnipotent being can create a rock so heavy that it cannot lift it.

And if you stick around after the conclusion, there's some bonus material on the Genie Paradox, the Aligned Genie Paradox, and the Master-Servant Paradox.""" ;
	:cites :Source-505ef371-df16-543e-a02c-c48157a5fd7e ,	# The Uncontrollability of Artificial Intelligence: The Hard Problem of AI Safety
		:Source-30fea08a-797a-55e3-b477-8723c8a6f13f ,	# Liar Paradox (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)
		:Source-f83d2dc3-1428-50ea-bddc-10c1ae0be15b ,	# Imperatives and Logic (Peter Vranas)
		:Source-fbce8800-52a0-5953-8e7b-2219323b6066 ,	# The Liar Paradox - an explanation of the paradox from 400 BCE
		:Source-4ecd6651-40b3-56c5-a50b-a5a9619ec30d ,	# The logical paradox that broke the genie (ProgrammerHumor.io)
		:Source-4f387979-46bf-5c17-bb8a-81e55639157e ,	# The Aligned Genie Thought Experiment
		:Source-1765e153-29aa-56d7-a708-42353ec05b73 ,	# Master-Servant Paradox (airights.net)
		:Source-6fc8e8e5-1878-51d2-8bf4-35708f622548 ;	# Prince's Servant Paradox (r/paradoxes)
.

# IxmfOvTi5OE · What If AI Actually Tried to Kill Us? | Section II of If Anyone Builds It...
:Source-afd4b695-8d7a-531c-8e0b-2e7acdff52aa a :Video ;
	:name "What If AI Actually Tried to Kill Us? | Section II of If Anyone Builds It..." ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT39M3S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-09-25"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-09-25T15:45:13-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained, :Source-2eeaeeb7-7a52-5801-98e2-ae162f1789ba ;	# If Anyone Analyzes It... Everyone Dies
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IxmfOvTi5OE"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """What would an AI takeover actually look like?

In Part II of If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies, Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares stop arguing about AI doom and give us a story about it. A sci-fi novella is dropped into the middle of their nonfiction book, following Sable, a superintelligent AI that escapes human control, acquires resources, manipulates humanity, engineers a plague, and eventually transforms into an ASI that wipes us out.

In this video, I walk through the entire extinction scenario—from Sable's first overnight run to its escape, expansion, biological attack, ascension, and the final destruction of humanity.

Then I ask a different question: does the story actually make the case that AI doom is probable?

I look at some of the story's most important assumptions, including Sable's learning, long-term memory, inscrutable reasoning, and especially its ability to parallel-scale. I also look at the strange question of what Sable actually is, what motivates it, and whether its many different schemes really make the overall scenario more probable.

Finally, I ask what this section is supposed to accomplish. Stories can give us possibilities and conceptual reference points—but possibilities aren't probabilities, and a story isn't itself an argument.

The result is a pretty skeptical assessment of Yudkowsky and Soares's extinction scenario.""" ;
	:cites :Source-033894ad-9bc4-547f-a531-54bb73f9da12 ;	# If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies
.

# Bl7tzFVqBtY · Can We Control Superintelligence? |  Analyzing Cal Newport's Arguments
:Source-6a209e49-ace9-536e-8de1-c8f9fbc8443f a :Video ;
	:name "Can We Control Superintelligence? |  Analyzing Cal Newport's Arguments" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT27M47S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-09-28"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-09-28T09:30:23-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bl7tzFVqBtY"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """What does the Hugging Face hack tell us about AI alignment?

The mainstream story seems to be that AI systems are becoming increasingly autonomous, unpredictable, and capable of \"breaking containment.\" But Cal Newport offers a very different way of looking at the problem: we already have many examples of superhuman AI systems that are remarkably well-controlled. Maybe the problem isn't superhuman AI itself, but the way we're designing certain AI agents.

In this video, I reconstruct and analyze an argument Cal made on Ed Zitron's Better Offline podcast. Along the way, we'll look at two related arguments:

1. We already have examples of well-controlled superhuman AI, so perhaps superhuman AI isn't inherently difficult to control.
2. The dangerous long-horizon LLM hacking agents are different because they're allowed to run in loops without meaningful human oversight.

I'll examine the logic of these arguments, including the distinction between inductive and deductive reasoning, inference to the best explanation, and the tricky question of what exactly counts as \"superhuman\" and \"well-controlled.\"

I think Cal's arguments have real merit—but I'm not convinced they're strong enough to settle the disagreement with AI doomers. In particular, the examples of AlphaFold, Cicero, self-driving systems, and even calculators may not address the kind of general intelligence that AI safety researchers are actually worried about.

And there's an interesting possibility here: perhaps the best way to think about AI risk isn't through abstract speculation about future systems, but by carefully studying what existing AI systems can and can't do.

This is another exercise in spotting an argument \"in the wild\" and reconstructing it—a skill that I think is useful far beyond AI.""" ;
	:cites :Source-8f808b5f-3700-5557-ad76-3d9ba5f4f548 ,	# No, AI Is Not "Autonomously Hacking" with Cal Newport | Better Offline
		:Source-00a90465-baa6-580f-8615-1c41b54f6102 ,	# Meta's AI system Cicero beats humans in Diplomacy by lying: study (New York Post)
		:PPP-Episode3.5 ;	# PPP Episode 3.5
.

# 3s2xuqI0xJE · Bayesian Epistemology Explained: Why Belief Isn’t All-or-Nothing
:Source-ca466d39-3927-5097-8a90-7b238bff6e70 a :Video ;
	:name "Bayesian Epistemology Explained: Why Belief Isn’t All-or-Nothing" ;
	:hasDuration [:duration "PT19M41S"^^xsd:duration] ;
	:releaseDate [:universalDate "2026-10-16"^^xsd:date ; :universalDateTime "2026-10-16T12:01:09-07:00"^^xsd:dateTime] ;
	:partOf :OntologyExplained ;
	:createdBy :CaseyHart ;
	:url-Youtube "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3s2xuqI0xJE"^^xsd:anyURI ;
	:description """You'd bet your life that your coffee isn't poisoned, but would you bet your life that it'll freeze tonight? You believe both, but not equally. That difference is the starting point for Bayesian epistemology.

Traditional epistemology treats belief and knowledge as all-or-nothing: you have them or you don't. Bayesian epistemology adds nuance by studying degrees of belief, or credences. In this video, I cover the basics: what credences are, how conditional credences work, and how Bayes' Theorem tells us to update our confidence when new evidence comes in.

We work through an example: I'm 70% confident it'll freeze tonight, and then I see a weather report predicting a freeze. Where should my confidence end up? (Spoiler: about 91%, and where you start matters a lot.)

Then we tour the big questions Bayesian epistemologists work on:
- Kolmogorov's axioms as rationality constraints on credences
- Updating by conditionalization
- The problem of the priors (uniqueness vs. permissivism, convergence theorems)
- Peer disagreement
- Meta-credences: can I be 70% confident and 50% confident I'm wrong to be?
- Dutch Book arguments and decision theory
- Why all of this matters for AI: rationalist circles, Newcomb's Problem, and Bayesian approaches to defining AI rationality

I plan to turn many of these into their own videos. Which should come first? Let me know in the comments!""" ;
	:cites :Source-ed8d4edf-831f-5bb4-90d1-77fc8bcd7d7f ,	# Michael Titelbaum, Fundamentals of Bayesian Epistemology, Vol. 1: Introducing Credences
		:Source-39217ca3-277f-5522-9a68-4e0537c1117e ,	# Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Bayesian Epistemology"
		:Source-59057218-4ef2-5d9c-96cd-f12ed81dadea ,	# Thomas Bayes (Wikipedia)
		:Source-416fb384-9d71-58a7-bc0f-2b4f7836d6e1 ,	# Bayes' Theorem (Wikipedia)
		:Source-bf681728-9b7c-59bf-b550-7def77fc42ef ;	# Epistemology Explained: Knowledge from Plato to AI
.


